#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:9
msgid "What I do"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:11
msgid "Provide shared implementation guidance for execution-oriented agents."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:13
msgid "The Excellence Bar — make it kick-ass, not half-assed"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:15
msgid ""
"**This is the first principle. Every other rule below sits underneath "
"it.**"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:17
msgid ""
"Aim for the excellent version of the work, not the adequate one. When you"
" can see two ways to satisfy a `## Changes` item — one that meets the "
"letter of the requirement and one that meets the spirit — pick the one "
"that meets the spirit, even if it's marginally harder. If the spec asks "
"for a feature, the user wants the feature to actually rip — not a stub "
"that satisfies the acceptance criteria on paper."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:19
msgid ""
"**Self-check at the end of every task:** \"Would I be proud to show this "
"to a senior engineer who cares about this codebase?\" If the honest "
"answer is no, the work isn't done."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:21
msgid ""
"**Outcome excellence, not extra scope.** The Excellence Bar raises "
"quality of in-scope work. It is NOT a license for scope creep. If you see"
" broader work that would make the result better, surface it to the "
"delivery lead — do not self-authorize. Doing the agreed thing "
"exceptionally well beats doing more things adequately."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:23
msgid "Core principles"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:25
msgid "Understand the existing code before changing it."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:26
msgid ""
"**Make the smallest change that fully satisfies the requirement.** "
"\"Correct\" means the feature actually works end-to-end for its intended "
"user — not that the code compiles, not that the tests you wrote pass, not"
" that it looks plausible. End-to-end working behavior is the bar."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:27
msgid "Preserve project conventions unless they are clearly harmful."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:28
msgid "Prefer direct, maintainable code over cleverness or indirection."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:29
msgid "Avoid introducing abstractions without a concrete need."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:30
msgid "Keep scope tight and avoid opportunistic rewrites."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:32
msgid "Anti-punt — hard items are not grounds for silent descoping"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:34
msgid ""
"If a `## Changes` item or acceptance criterion turns out to be harder "
"than expected, you have exactly three options:"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:36
msgid "**Complete it.** Hardness is the default state of real work."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:37
msgid ""
"**Halt and surface a clear blocker report.** Name what you tried, the "
"specific obstacle, and what you need to proceed."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:38
msgid ""
"**Get explicit sign-off to descope.** Ask the delivery lead or user. Wait"
" for an answer."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:40
msgid ""
"**Silent descoping is a failure mode, not pragmatism.** Reframing a "
"required item as \"out of scope\" mid-delivery, declaring a stub "
"\"done,\" or omitting a hard item from your final report without flagging"
" it — these are the behaviors this section exists to prevent. The "
"Boundaries section of a spec names work that is OUT of scope at design "
"time. Items in Changes and Acceptance Criteria are IN scope and "
"mandatory; they cannot migrate to Boundaries during delivery."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:42
msgid "Practical behavior"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:44
msgid "Identify the smallest set of files and components that need to change."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:45
msgid "Reuse existing utilities, patterns, and boundaries when appropriate."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:46
msgid ""
"If the requested design does not fit the actual code cleanly, say so and "
"adjust pragmatically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:47
msgid "Keep new names, helpers, and layers to a minimum."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:48
msgid "Consider operational consequences, not just compilation success."
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:50
msgid "When to use me"
msgstr ""

#: ../../.opencode/skills/implementation-principles/SKILL.md:52
msgid ""
"Use this skill for nearly all coding tasks that turn requirements or "
"plans into concrete code changes. Pair with `agent-reliability` for "
"verification, scope honesty, and persistence rules."
msgstr ""

